    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/PUBDOC2447_document.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2009-12-08-2009-12-08-0168",
    "chunk_index": 168,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "3ccff6b94549421f7edf2c26cdd542cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "I wish to say a few things in regard to certain matters of utmost importance to the Tamil people, particularly to the persons who have been displaced in the Vanni. It would appear from the information available, that maybe around 50 per cent of the people who were originally confined to camps have left the camps, but there are still around 45 per cent of them in the camps.\n\nThe impression is also sought to be made that when the people are sent out of the camps it is a great achievement and that everything is all right thereafter. I am sorry that we have had to point out several times in the past and we have to point out now that these persons, before they were displaced consequent to the war, led honourable lives. They were in a position to live happily and peacefully on their lands, in their houses; they generated their income; they looked after their families. Today, they have lost their houses; they have lost all that they owned in this country and they have been reduced to a state of penury, destitution. Therefore, merely releasing them from the camps is not sufficient. These people have got to be rehabilitated. Their houses have got to be constructed. In many instances, the houses have been damaged beyond repair. Their livelihood must be restored. They must be in a position to commence their occupation. They must be in a position to generate an income with which they can look after their families.\n\nWe have always been complaining that the Government has no comprehensive plan in regard to these matters. It has been our complaint that the Government has no road map; the Government has not made its positions clear. Why can the Government not be transparent and let everybody know what is happening; what their plans are; what they are going to do with these people; how they are going to be rehabilitated; what is going to be done for them in regard to housing; in regard to livelihood opportunities and so on and so forth?"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2000s",
    "doc_id": "2009-12-08-2009-12-08",
    "num": "2009-12-08",
    "date_str": "2009-12-08",
    "description": "Hansard of December 08, 2009",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/PUBDOC2447_document.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2009-12-08-2009-12-08-0169",
    "chunk_index": 169,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "27a2eb80280e9028aec0546ec5787401",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1732,
    "chunk_text": "We have always been complaining that the Government has no comprehensive plan in regard to these matters. It has been our complaint that the Government has no road map; the Government has not made its positions clear. Why can the Government not be transparent and let everybody know what is happening; what their plans are; what they are going to do with these people; how they are going to be rehabilitated; what is going to be done for them in regard to housing; in regard to livelihood opportunities and so on and so forth?\n\nINGOs funded by foreign governments are prepared to help lavishly in regard to rehabilitating and resettling these people. But, we are inclined to the view that many of these countries are holding back the assistance that they can give, on account of the fact that the Government has no definite plan in regard to resettlement and the Government\u2019s lack of transparency in regard to this whole question is being looked at with misgiving by many persons who can play a very useful role in looking after these people who need much assistance.\n\nThe second point I want to make, is that there were some High Security Zones in the Northern Province and the Eastern Province and these zones continue to be effective and are still being maintained as High Security Zones though the war has come to an end. It is our submission that there is no justification whatever for these High Security Zones to continue any longer. The rationale for these High Security Zones was that the LTTE had artillery fire and they fired with artillery at certain institutions like the Palaly Airport. To ensure that LTTE artillery fire would not reach these institutions as the airport a zone was declared as a High Security Zone."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2000s",
