    "description": "Hansard of December 08, 2009",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/PUBDOC2447_document.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2009-12-08-2009-12-08-0170",
    "chunk_index": 170,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "d5dacbe339f1dbd0e1734509ce40aa54",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1408,
    "chunk_text": "The second point I want to make, is that there were some High Security Zones in the Northern Province and the Eastern Province and these zones continue to be effective and are still being maintained as High Security Zones though the war has come to an end. It is our submission that there is no justification whatever for these High Security Zones to continue any longer. The rationale for these High Security Zones was that the LTTE had artillery fire and they fired with artillery at certain institutions like the Palaly Airport. To ensure that LTTE artillery fire would not reach these institutions as the airport a zone was declared as a High Security Zone.\n\nIn Valikamam North in the Jaffna District, the people who were displaced as far back as 1989 and 1990 on account of the High Security Zone - for almost for the past 20 years, two long decades - have not been able to live on their lands. Twenty eight thousand families consisting of about 100,000 people were displaced from the High Security Zone. The vast majority of them, about 60 per cent, were people depending on agriculture on land in the High Security Zone. There were 45 Grama Sevaka Divisions covered by the High Security Zone, an entire territory of 59.5 square kilometres, about 60 square\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0da9\u0db6\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0dc0\u0dca. \u0da9\u0dd3. \u0dd9\u0da2\u0dca. \u0dd9\u0dc3\u0dd9\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dd2\u0dbb\u0dad\u0dca\u0db1 \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf]\n\n2009 \u0dd9\u0daf\u0dc3\u0dd0\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 08 1573 1574"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2000s",
